-- X12 (#2185, S0) THE COMPILER'S OWN SYNTHESIZED occurrence, not a user one --
-- the corpus had none of these. x6/x8 grade what a user-WRITTEN bare `println`
-- denotes when shadowed; this row grades what the AUTO-PRINT WRAP's own
-- COMPILER-SYNTHESIZED `println` denotes when the SAME shadow is in scope but
-- the source text never writes `println` at all. `main = 7` is a bare non-Unit
-- VALUE main, so `driver/main_autoprint.mdk` rewrites it before elaboration;
-- the only question this fixture asks is what THAT synthetic reference means.
--
-- MEASURED ON THE BASE BINARY (93a40382, cold-built in this worktree,
-- MEDAKA_STRICT=1): check/build both ACCEPT (exit 0) and the BUILT BINARY
-- prints 1007 -- the wrap's synthesized `println` silently dispatched through
-- THIS module's `impl Ifc Int`, adding 1000 to the value on its way out. No
-- diagnostic anywhere; a right-looking wrong number at exit 0 (S0 shape).
-- FIXED shape (this slice): the wrap no longer spells the name `println` at
-- all (`putStrLn (display <e>)`, byte-identical to `println`'s own prelude
-- body), so it is not capturable by ANY interface named `println` -- the
-- built binary must print 7, the value `main` actually holds.
--
-- `run` REJECTs BY DESIGN (#1681, mirrors x7/x11): `main` is a non-Unit VALUE
-- and `medaka run` never applies or prints one, regardless of this fixture's
-- shadow -- graded BUILD_EXACT for the same reason x7 is. Value hand-derived
-- from the literal `main = 7` (the impl's `+ 1000` must NEVER fire here), not
-- captured.
interface Ifc c where
  println : c -> Int

impl Ifc Int where
  println n = n + 1000

main = 7
